description:
src/memory.rs:441-446 and src/memory.rs:861-865 are byte-identical:

    if let Some(max) = max_changes {
        if relevant.len() as u64 > max {
            return Err(BackendChangesError::TooManyChanges { limit: max });
        }
    }

Both branches are also preceded by identical 'parse since_state into u64 via TooManyChanges{limit:0}' (lines 427-430 and 846-849) — same anti-pattern (using TooManyChanges{limit:0} as a misuse of the variant for a parse error; that is its own correctness issue, separate finding worthy elsewhere).

What goes wrong: any change to the cap-check logic (e.g. adding a maxSize-aware tally, switching from len() to a streamed scan) must be applied twice. Drift between the default-impl path (get_changes) and the override path (get_metadata_changes) is the exact failure mode the canonical-template propagation rule guards against, but at the intra-crate scope: the two impls already drift if one is updated and the other is not.

Idiomatic fix: extract a private helper that walks the change_log Vec for a given type_name + account + since_state + max_changes and returns the relevant entries (or a TooManyChanges error). Both impls call this helper, and the cap check lives in one place. The Metadata-specific override adds only the per-record filter logic on top.

Note: this is a clean-up; the canonical template (jmap-mail-server) does not have a get_metadata_changes equivalent so this DRY-up is metadata-server-specific. Cookie-cutter propagation does not apply here.
